<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muscogee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Creek people, Muscogee Creek, Creek, Creek Confederacy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muscogee, or Creek, are a Native American people of the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, whose towns in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> formed a powerful confederacy in the 18th century. They traded and fought with English, Spanish and French colonists. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a> of 1813–1814 they lost much land, and most were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in the 1830s, where the Muscogee Nation is based.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscogee.jpg" alt="Muscogee" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hodge, Frederick Webb (editor). Handbook of American Indians North of Mexico in Two Parts: Part I. Smithsonian Instit... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creek_man_p_363_from_Handbook_of_American_Indians_north_of_Mexico_by_Hodge.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Muscogee, or Mvskoke, are a group of related Indigenous peoples of the Southeastern Woodlands. Their historical homelands lie in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and parts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Their ancestors were part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian cultures</a>, which between 800 and 1600 CE built complex cities with earthwork mounds, surrounded by satellite towns and farmsteads. The Muscogee are associated with mound sites such as Ocmulgee, Etowah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a>.</p><p>Spanish explorers met their ancestors in the mid-16th century. The Spanish expedition brought epidemics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a>, and the Indian slave trade conducted by colonists in the 17th and 18th centuries added to the losses. As survivors and their descendants regrouped, the Muscogee Creek Confederacy arose as a loose alliance of Muskogee-speaking peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The English founded Charles Town in 1670, and traders from Carolina visited Muscogee towns to exchange firearms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, axes, glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, cloth and rum for deerskins and Indian slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> built missions, while the British and French preferred trade to conversion. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> the Muscogee leaned towards the British, but, divided by factions, generally avoided sustained fighting. Many leaders of the period, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-mcgillivray">Alexander McGillivray</a> and William McIntosh, bore British names through intermarriage.</p><p>In 1811 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> visited Tuckabatchee, telling the Muscogee that a comet signalled his coming. Upper Towns, inspired by him and by their own religious leaders, resisted American encroachment. The result was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Red Stick War</a>, or Creek War, of 1813-1814, which began as a civil war and became entangled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> put down the rebellion with the help of Southern Muscogee, and the nation was forced to sign the Treaty of Fort Jackson on 9 August 1814, ceding land even from those who had fought beside him.</p><p>Under Jackson's presidency from 1829 the government pursued the removal of all tribes east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>. In the 1830s most of the Muscogee were forcibly relocated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. A small group remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, and others had earlier moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where they helped form the Seminole.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Muscogee are matrilineal: each person belongs to the clan of their mother, and inheritance and property pass through the maternal line. Hereditary chiefs were born into certain clans. Town networks were laid out around plazas, ballparks and square ceremonial dance grounds. The Stomp Dance and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn Ceremony</a> remain revered gatherings, and stickball games, festivals and language classes continue to sustain a tribal identity.</p><p>The Muscogee language belongs to the Eastern Muskogean branch of the Muskogean family. Today the Muscogee (Creek) Nation is headquartered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okmulgee">Okmulgee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, and the Poarch Band of Creek Indians is federally recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muscogee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maize, worked with hoes of Mill <b>Creek</b> chert quarried in southern Illinois, became a staple alongside native seed crops such as goosefoot and sumpweed, squash, and the fish, waterfowl and deer of the river bottoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Creek War some 500 Cherokees fought beside Andrew Jackson against the Red Stick <b>Creeks</b> at the Battle of Horseshoe Bend in March 1814, John Ross among them, a service that brought them no protection when Jackson later pressed for removal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Texans killed Comanche leaders at a council in San Antonio in March 1840, Buffalo Hump led a great raid to the coast at Linnville, and Texan forces attacked the returning column at Plum <b>Creek</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish men married or had unions with Pensacola, <b>Creek</b> and African women, slave and free, and their children formed a mestizo and mulatto population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chunkey, a game in which players threw poles after a rolling stone disc, spread from the Cahokia region with Mississippian ways; <b>Creek</b>, Choctaw and Chickasaw men still played it in the 18th century, and the Cherokee have revived it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 December 1890, at Wounded Knee <b>Creek</b> near the Pine Ridge Agency, soldiers trying to disarm the Miniconjou band of Spotted Elk opened fire, with carbines and Hotchkiss guns on the hill above the camp.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance songs</a></li></ul></section>
